WEBVTT

00:00:03.033 --> 00:00:11.571
Open remotefs in any browser
The service runs on a machine that can reach your storage. You just need a browser.

00:00:14.645 --> 00:00:22.808
One window for everything
Local folders, network shares, cloud buckets and VM storage live in the sidebar.

00:00:39.654 --> 00:00:46.442
Feels like Finder
Click a folder to open it. Breadcrumbs and Back take you up again.

00:00:48.565 --> 00:00:58.165
Tick to select, Shift-click for a range
Just like a Mac list view: pick one file, then Shift-click to grab everything in between.

00:00:58.234 --> 00:01:04.647
⌘-click to add or drop one file
Fine-tune the selection without starting over.

00:01:07.965 --> 00:01:14.565
Drag to select
Draw a box across the list. Rows light up as the box touches them.

00:01:27.797 --> 00:01:35.834
Right-click for everything else
Download, zip, copy, cut, rename, shortlist and delete, all in one menu.

00:01:45.026 --> 00:01:51.626
Get Info on any file
Size, type and modification time, straight from the storage.

00:01:55.942 --> 00:02:00.408
Keyboard shortcuts work too
⌘A selects all. ⌘C, ⌘X and ⌘V copy, cut and paste. ⌘/ lists the rest.

00:02:00.458 --> 00:02:06.120
Filter and sort
Type to narrow the folder. Click a column to sort.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:28.135
Make a folder
New folder is in the toolbar and on the right-click menu.

00:02:32.701 --> 00:02:39.738
Copy, then paste somewhere else
Select files, copy them, open the destination and paste.

00:03:09.509 --> 00:03:14.047
Rename in place
Right-click, Rename, type. Done.

00:03:21.687 --> 00:03:30.099
Open and edit text files
Notes, CSVs and Markdown open in a built-in editor and save back to the same storage.

00:03:58.072 --> 00:04:06.235
Find every file server on your network
Scan network probes the subnets you allow for SMB and NFS services.

00:04:17.526 --> 00:04:26.876
Names, not just addresses
Each device shows its DNS name, NetBIOS name and IP, so you can tell the NAS from the reception PC.

00:04:36.648 --> 00:04:45.311
Map a share in two clicks
Sign in with the NAS account. Tick Save to keep the login encrypted on the service host.

00:05:03.007 --> 00:05:12.544
Shares appear in the sidebar
Browsing needs no drive letters or mount commands. Mount a share as a drive only when you want one.

00:05:35.554 --> 00:05:45.216
Right-click a server for its options
List shares, reconnect as someone else, copy its address, forget the saved login, or unmount.

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:55.608
Unmount when you are done
The connection closes and your files stay put on the server.

00:05:55.658 --> 00:06:03.008
Saved logins reconnect in one click
The scan shows which servers already have a stored login.

00:06:29.736 --> 00:06:38.399
Map as many servers as you like
Each server keeps its own login. Here the backup box joins the NAS in the sidebar.

00:07:23.482 --> 00:07:31.207
Keep favourite folders one click away
Star a folder, or right-click it and choose Shortlist folder.

00:07:58.887 --> 00:08:07.049
It remembers across sessions
Shortlisted network folders reopen with the saved login, even after a reload.

00:08:12.199 --> 00:08:18.736
Right-click a shortlist entry to manage it
Open it, copy its path, or forget it.

00:08:27.593 --> 00:08:37.506
Add cloud storage from the sidebar
S3 and S3-compatible storage, Backblaze B2, Dropbox, Google Drive, OneDrive, WebDAV and Azure Blob.

00:08:54.245 --> 00:09:00.407
Copy from your computer…
Select a folder and copy it, just like in Finder.

00:09:05.269 --> 00:09:12.931
…and paste into the cloud
The service moves the bytes directly. Nothing goes through your browser.

00:09:19.828 --> 00:09:25.597
Folders, images and documents arrive intact
Open files in the bucket to check them.

00:09:25.647 --> 00:09:34.559
The same trick works for network shares
Copy from S3 onto the NAS (via the shortlist), or between any two connections.

00:09:42.817 --> 00:09:52.667
Mount a share as a folder or drive
Right-click a share or folder and choose Mount on this computer. Here: a client folder on the NAS.

00:10:01.756 --> 00:10:11.544
Read-write by default
Name the mount, and untick Allow changes if it should be read-only. Windows also lets you pick a drive letter.

00:10:13.546 --> 00:10:21.367
It shows up under Mounted on this computer
Linux and macOS get a folder under ~/remotefs; Windows gets a drive letter for your account only.

00:10:21.417 --> 00:10:29.580
Cloud storage mounts too, read-only if you like
Open the S3 connection, mount it and untick Allow changes.

00:10:56.777 --> 00:11:06.502
Every app sees an ordinary folder
Open, save and copy with any program. There is no file-size limit, so video and disk images work.

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:21.435
Read-only means read-only
The S3 mount opens files but refuses changes.

00:11:34.027 --> 00:11:42.502
Changes land on the NAS
The service uploads in the background. The dot turns green when everything has arrived.

00:11:53.523 --> 00:12:01.810
Eject when you are done
Eject waits for uploads to finish. The service also ejects its mounts when it stops.

00:12:09.327 --> 00:12:18.114
Download big folders in parts
Zip a large selection on the server and split it into parts that are easy to download.

00:12:20.879 --> 00:12:29.416
Choose where the ZIP is built and how big each part is
Packing happens on the server, using its disk, not yours.

00:12:44.248 --> 00:12:50.759
Pause and resume packing
Jobs keep running on the server even if you close this view.

00:12:50.809 --> 00:12:59.971
Download each part, then free the space
Parts resume if a download drops. Purge removes the staged copy when you are done.

00:13:20.398 --> 00:13:28.873
See your VM storage too
Connect to a hypervisor the administrator has configured, and browse its storage pools.

00:13:40.006 --> 00:13:47.918
Volumes, sizes and formats at a glance
Read-only inventory: remotefs never opens or changes a VM disk.

00:14:11.365 --> 00:14:17.227
Everything in the app works from the terminal
The remotefs command talks to the same service, so scripts and cron jobs get the same storage.

00:14:17.277 --> 00:14:22.888
Scan the network
Same scan as the Scan button: DNS name, NetBIOS name and address for every file server.

00:14:22.938 --> 00:14:31.600
List shares with the login saved in the app
Credentials stay encrypted on the service. The CLI only uses their ID.

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:40.070
Connect, list and read files
connect returns a session ID; ls, get, put, copy, rename and remove all take it.

00:14:44.656 --> 00:14:53.506
Copy between storage, from a script
Copy a file from this machine straight onto the NAS. The service moves the bytes.

00:14:57.997 --> 00:15:08.347
Cloud connections, ZIP jobs and favourites too
remotes, downloads and saved cover the rest of the app. Run remotefs --help for the full list.

00:15:25.901 --> 00:15:31.621
Drop the picker into your own app
The repo ships the templates: mount the API inside your FastAPI app and add one custom element.

00:15:31.671 --> 00:15:40.833
Mount the API and add the element
Your login protects it through an authenticate hook. No React, Vue or build step needed.

00:15:50.714 --> 00:15:56.939
Your users pick a folder…
Here a job tracker asks where a job’s files live.

00:15:59.506 --> 00:16:06.202
…and your app gets a descriptor, never a password
Store it with your record. The service resolves access when the folder is opened again.
